[common]
displayName="DeepSeek Harness"
desc="The open-source, plugin-based coding agent from DeepSeek AI. It serves the DSH Web interface through the fnOS unified gateway and runs on the DSH 0.2.2-rc.4 baseline.<br /><br /><strong>Highlights</strong><br />1. Reach DSH through the NAS Web unified gateway; the service listens on 127.0.0.1 only by default.<br />2. Ships the fnOS plugin; Codex Auth and Failover Search are no longer bundled since 8.1.4 and can be installed from the Discover page or the plugin detail page (versions follow the DSH 0.2.2-rc.4 baseline and compatibility is judged by the new rules); upgrades keep credentials, model settings, sessions and workspaces.<br />3. Grants NAS directory access and remembers authorized directories (kept across upgrades and restarts, pruned automatically once a permission lapses); supports <code>/fn</code> file references, attachments, session log export and opening files in their apps. The fnOS icon above the input area opens a wide panel in place of the command palette, sized to the input box: create folders and empty files, right-click to open or rename an entry (folders open in the file manager, files in fnOS preview), and reopening the panel restores the last folder and scroll position.<br />4. The session log menu matches DSH upstream: under &quot;Download Session log&quot; you can export to your computer or to the NAS, plus a &quot;Feedback&quot; entry pointing at this repository.<br />5. Bundles the <code>fnos-cli</code> Agent Skill (with its own launcher that downloads and verifies the matching CLI version on first use); if you already installed it yourself it is skipped rather than overwritten.<br />6. Provides an interactive terminal in the sidebar that picks an available bash and injects a UTF-8 environment.<br />7. <strong>Plugin management</strong> happens in-app: installed plugins render as cards you can enable/disable, update or uninstall (with in-place confirmation); installs and updates pick the version matching the current DSH baseline, run through pnpm and stream logs live; cards flag &quot;new version X.Y.Z&quot;. The toolbox adds <strong>npm mirror management</strong> (bundled npm/yarn/cnpm/tencent/taobao/npmMirror/huawei sources, one-click switch), <strong>custom plugin indexes</strong> (add several sources and browse them on the Discover page) and <strong>fix plugin install issues</strong> (clears stale release-age exemptions, rebuilds profile dependencies and reinstalls managed plugins; the profile config is backed up into the app share's backup folder first, and the per-step log stays on the card). Third-party plugin API gateway prefixes are detected automatically; plugins use the npm registry configured in the app's <code>HOME/.npmrc</code>; after an uninstall that keeps data, reinstalling restores the reverse-proxy configuration from cache automatically.<br />8. Publishes a plugin market index: add the source on the DSH &quot;Discover&quot; page to browse and install all seven plugins, with localized names, descriptions and official icons. The Runtime page shows CPU/memory/disk/network in a four-tile layout with charts, plus this app's process tree and per-process termination.<br />9. Install, upgrade and runtime configuration stay idempotent: upgrades migrate stale prompt-variable references, and failures keep existing user data; &quot;trusted access address&quot; is validated the same way in the install, upgrade and config wizards and only accepts a host or host:port (several allowed, comma-separated), so a bad value is rejected on the spot instead of surfacing later as &quot;check Node.js dependencies&quot; after an app that will not start.<br /><br /><strong>Shortcuts</strong><br />1. <code>F5</code>, <code>Ctrl + R</code> on Windows/Linux or <code>⌘ + R</code> on macOS: refresh the DSH Web iframe only.<br />2. <code>Ctrl + ,</code> on Windows/Linux or <code>⌘ + ,</code> on macOS: open the DSH settings panel.<br />3. While picking a NAS file under <code>/fn</code>, press <code>Tab</code> to enter a subdirectory.<br />4. In the third-party plugin API URL reverse-proxy settings, press <code>Ctrl/⌘ + S</code> to save.<br /><br /><strong>Installation notes</strong><br />The package comes in x86 and ARM builds with the platform in the file name; pick the one matching your machine (app center refuses to install a package for the wrong platform). The ARM build has not been verified on real hardware by the publisher and is community-tested. The install wizard configures the port, the trusted access address and an npm registry; the listen address is always 127.0.0.1. Enter only a host or host:port for &quot;trusted access address&quot; (for example <code>192.0.2.10:5666</code>), without <code>http://</code> or a path, separated by commas, and wrap IPv6 addresses in brackets. If installation fails, check <code>/var/log/apps/fn-deepseek-harness.log</code> first, keep your existing app data, then retry."
changelog="This update moves the runtime baseline to <strong>DSH 0.2.2-rc.4</strong> and tidies the session header and Session-log menu entries.<br /><br /><strong>Baseline upgrade</strong><br />1. The runtime baseline goes from 0.2.2-rc.1 across rc.2 and rc.3 straight to <strong>0.2.2-rc.4</strong>; the seven bundled plugins and the install callback are aligned, and plugins load normally on the new runtime.<br />2. <strong>One baseline everywhere</strong>: the default catalog, the named catalog, the release-age exclusion list and the install-callback constant now share one source, so the install can no longer end up with a new baseline on top while dependencies still resolve against the old one. Guard tests fail on any single missed spot.<br />3. Fixed the official detector mistaking the system's <code>/usr/sbin/zed</code> (the ZFS event daemon) for the Zed editor, which used to expose an entry that could never open.<br /><br /><strong>Session header and log menu</strong><br />4. The session header now keeps <strong>one "More actions" button</strong>. Previously the official one and ours sat side by side with identical looks; they are merged, and the tooltip matches the official wording.<br />5. <strong>File manager</strong> moves into that menu's <strong>"Open with"</strong> submenu, using the colored NAS file-manager icon. The row gets separators above and below and a taller hit area, setting it apart from in-app actions such as copy and download.<br />6. <strong>Exporting the Session log no longer shows two dialogs</strong>: the official prompt and ours used to appear together; the official one now carries the feedback alone.<br /><br /><strong>Install wizard</strong><br />7. The retired npmMirror entry (a read-only archive endpoint that always failed) is gone from the wizard; existing configurations pointing at it are switched back to the official source automatically.<br />8. The upgrade wizard preselects no source — leaving it empty means the official npm registry. Fresh installs and app settings still default to the official source. Failures now show the registry actually in use.<br /><br /><strong>Other</strong><br />9. Plugin detail install instructions now show the current baseline version instead of a stale one."

